`context/renders/materials/<syllabus>/<concept>.<lang>.md` is a **material**: the guide a
learner studies. `<concept>.<lang>.slide.md` next to it is the deck. It is derived from a wiki
page and its lesson but governed by neither lane: no frontmatter, no kind, no lint, no
derivation gate. Its first line is the header `sdt context wiki render --check` reads.

Read this module when you write or revise a guide, after `instructions/lesson.md` (the
example and the transfer) and `instructions/practice.md` (the cases). `instructions/course.md`
owns the sequence.

## The brief

You do not start from the wiki page. You start from an immutable, versioned brief with these
fields:

- **Reader**: either an exact recipient profile id and revision, or the material-specific
  knowledge, terminology, register, examples and accessibility needs. A profile is optional for
  an occasional brief.
- **Outcome**: what the reader will be able to decide, explain or do after the material.
- **Content scope**: the claims and objectives that belong in this material.
- **Sources**: version-bound claims, objectives, lesson and reviewed cases. Record the claim
  digest or source revision used; do not rely on a mutable path alone.
- **Forms**: one or more forms chosen to serve the outcome.
- **Length**: an explicit word range and any other material-specific limits.
- **Overrides**: each profile override names its field, value and reason.
- **Teaching examples**: each invented teaching example states what source mechanism it
  preserves; label it explicitly rather than presenting its details as source facts.

Store a profile revision as `context/learning/profiles/<profile-id>/<revision>.yaml` and a brief
as `context/learning/briefs/<material-id>/<revision>.yaml`. A material id is its path under
`context/renders/materials`, without `.md`. Create and validate them through
`sdt material profile create|check` and `sdt material brief create|check`. Bind a completed guide
with `sdt material brief bind <material-id> <revision>`; the exact binding lives in its
`.meta.yaml` companion. A later brief revision never rewrites the one a material already cites.
Briefs and review sidecars are authoring data and are excluded from reader packages. A referenced
recipient profile may travel when it is needed to interpret the material and its rights allow the
package target.

You may also receive the existing guide. It is a source of facts and a trace, not a model to
follow: when it contradicts the brief, the brief wins. If a field is missing or two fields
contradict each other, resolve the conflict before writing. A repeated source reference with two
versions is a conflict, not a choice for the writer to guess.

## The shape

Choose the form from the outcome. Combine forms when the material needs more than one job; omit
sections that do not support that outcome.

- **Conceptual explanation** gives the mental model, mechanisms and boundaries needed to
  understand the concept.
- **Procedural guide** gives the ordered actions, branches and completion conditions needed to
  carry out a task.
- **Decision case** gives a concrete situation, evidence and constraints, then asks for a
  consequential choice.
- **Operational reference** gives the compact definitions, signals and actions needed at the
  point of work.

Use a worked example, a case, a retrieval prompt or an exercise only when it advances the stated
outcome. A short recall prompt is purposeful when retrieval supports later application or
retention; it is not a required section or a substitute for transfer. Never state what the page
lacks, and never name a successor that has no material.

## Style

- Sentences under 25 words, one idea each; paragraphs of two to four sentences.
- Second person singular, direct and sober. No emphatic adverbs and no rhetorical
  superlatives.
- A foreign term stays in its language, with the Italian gloss in parentheses at first use.
  Do not redefine a term the corpus uses without defining it.
- A general principle — a law, a maxim, a rule — appears only with the case that shows it, in
  the same section. Never alone, before the case.
- Every section answers a question the reader is actually asking, and its title says so.
- Use the cases' names and figures exactly as given.

## Facts

- The brief is the only source. Do not add figures, names, roles, motives, sectors or details
  it does not contain; do not give a concept a meaning or a property the brief does not give.
- You may connect facts with linking sentences. When the link is an inference, write it
  cautiously and report it at delivery.
- Do not draw from a case a conclusion stronger than its data. If the data do not say
  something got worse, do not write that it got worse.
- Do not repeat the same fact in two sections.
- Do not fix thresholds, values or criteria the brief leaves to the reader.

## Figures

A figure earns its place only when it carries a relation the prose cannot state in one
sentence. The medium follows the content:

- a relation, a flow, a sequence or a decomposition → a Mermaid fence;
- values over categories or time → `xychart`, with the same values in a table;
- parts of a whole → `pie`; points on two axes → `quadrantChart`; flows with quantities →
  `sankey`;
- a scatter whose points carry a size → a table; a positioning matrix → a small SVG;
- a dashboard → a table of its tiles and bands, never the screenshot;
- a screenshot, a photo or a decoration → left out, with the reason recorded.

**No values, no chart.** A chart whose values the brief does not carry is dropped, not
invented: the source's dashboard is its own system, and a guide that borrows its numbers
teaches the reader nothing about theirs. Cite the figure to the span that carries it; shape
defers to `instructions/diagram.md` when the project has one.

An image or screenshot must do a task-essential explanatory job. Before inclusion, verify its
rights, privacy, provenance and standalone availability; provide an accessible description that
states the information the image conveys. Do not include an original source figure or a decorative
image. If the image or its description cannot travel with the package, record the blocker.

## The binder

The text is written without markers. After the draft, a binder pass:

1. maps each sentence to a claim, stamping `<!-- claim=<page>#<id> digest=<sha> -->`;
2. marks the lesson's example and transfer with `<!-- lesson=<page>#worked-example -->` and
   `#transfer`, a gloss with `<!-- gloss=<term> -->`, and each objective section with
   `<!-- objective=N -->` exactly once;
3. lists the sentences that have no claim — the inferences and the case prose — so the delivery
   can say which they are;
4. writes the header on the first line: concept, syllabus, language, classification, and one
   claim id with its digest per asserted claim.

The writing context never sees the markers. `sdt context wiki render --check` is the gate on
the result. The canonical `<guide-complete-filename>.meta.yaml` companion binds the guide
and deck bytes and keeps per-artifact claims plus review provenance; legacy `.ledger.json`
files remain as migration inputs and are not deleted automatically.

Declare `privacy: none|personal|real` on artifacts and source assets; assets use
`<filename>.meta.yaml`. Set it with `sdt material privacy set|asset`. `personal`/`real` need
`--basis`; diagrams inherit privacy. Missing values block export.

## Checks before delivery

- Recompute every arithmetic operation and every reading of a percentage in words: 2 out of 36
  is about six per hundred, not two per hundred. Correct the errors inherited from the existing
  guide too.
- Check that the cases' sums add up.
- Count the words against the brief's range, and the sentences against 25 words.
- Check that every foreign term is glossed at first use.
- Check that no marker, HTML comment or LaTeX is left in the draft — the binder adds the first.
- Check that every fact appears in the brief; if not, remove it or flag it.
- Check that every selected source binding still resolves to the version named by the brief.
- Check that a retrieval prompt or exercise serves the outcome and does not reveal its answer.
- Check that required assets and their accessible descriptions resolve from the exported package.

Deliver the files, then three things: the final lengths, the errors corrected in the existing
version, and the places where you interpreted the facts beyond the brief, so the reviewer
knows where to look.

## The references

Use the project's transformed analogy and decisional case as tone references, never as sources.
Match their shape; critique names any register mismatch.
